From 01650f1ac255bd98819588407726f23280633f40 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:37:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(repair): give CI corrections bounded, fenced CI failure
 evidence

Live (2026-10-17 ~23:00Z): 39 of 48 open Sentinel PRs on ubiquity/ai.ubq.fi
are red on the `validate` job of Gateway CI, and the CI correction finding
named only the check ("no CI log text is available"). Their annotations
carry nothing useful (a Node.js 20 deprecation warning and "Process
completed with exit code N") and the check output is empty, so the job log
is the evidence. Example: PR #1009 failed only on a prettier warning for
src/provider/lithos-rate-limits.ts and eslint 81:9
sonarjs/no-nested-conditional, about 520 bytes into a 1.1 MB step.

The trusted matrix cell now collects the evidence when it prepares a CI
correction cell (post-review correction and pre-review CI fix alike: the CI
finding first, `checkoutBase` set, no `mergeBase`), after every admission
and reservation check and before its final start-bound check, within the
time the start can spare (never a `not_started` refusal):

- Reader (src/github/client-ci-evidence*.ts): repository-scoped GETs through
  `sendCore` (cooldown gate, auth, deadline) on the cell's read-only App
  token. Per failing check (at most 8 named, logs for at most 4): name,
  conclusion, app; for Actions jobs the workflow name and file, the job, its
  failing steps, annotations (at most 10) and a bounded excerpt of the job
  log, read through the existing trusted results-host check
  (`trustedActionsLogUrl`) without credentials in explicit 4 MiB ranges: the
  first 32 MiB, then the last 256 KiB with the gap marked. Non-Actions check
  runs give their output, commit statuses their description. 60 s and 64
  requests per correction; the first rate limit stops every later read; it
  never throws, and every failure degrades to a fixed reason code.
- Excerpt (src/repair/ci-evidence-excerpt.ts): generic first. A streaming
  scanner keeps the failing step(s) (`##[error]`, else the last step); a
  step that fits its share is kept whole. Otherwise its header and tail,
  windows around the strong failure markers above the tail that shrink to
  fit (40/8 down to bare markers, then first and last alternately), and the
  rest of the share filled nearest first: the markers' after-context, their
  before-context, the contiguous run after the first marker, weak windows
  (unrecognized failures only), then the step's start and end alternately.
  The scanner keeps each step's first and last 16 KiB and the 16 KiB after
  its first strong marker for this, so an unmarked key line in the middle
  of a step survives (a generated-file diff header, a Jest expected/received
  diff, the last line of a traceback). Formatter, linter, compiler and
  test-name hints are optional summaries.
- Sanitizer and fence (ci-evidence-sanitize.ts, ci-evidence-render.ts):
  timestamps, ANSI, control, bidi and zero-width characters removed, paths
  made repository-relative, credential shapes redacted (never 40/64-hex SHAs
  or paths), lines capped at 400 characters; the content sits between two
  markers with a fresh 128-bit nonce, scrubbed of the nonce, the marker
  label and code fences, under a header that it is UNTRUSTED output of the
  candidate's code, to be used only as a description of the failure.
- Classification (ci-evidence-classify.ts): advisory "candidate" or "likely
  infrastructure" label; correction and round policy are unchanged.
- Budgets: 32 KiB fenced content, 4-16 KiB per check, 36,000 characters for
  the section. Over the 39 live red heads every true error line is kept at
  a 16, 8 or 4 KiB share (small failing steps whole in about 500
  characters; the 1.9 MB logs fill about 15,000 of a 16 KiB share) and all
  39 are labelled "candidate".

Delivery is at cell time only: `runModel(request, { untrustedCiEvidence })`
carries the rendered section into the user turn's input only (the role
paragraph stays last); the Codex thread's system-level `baseInstructions`
keep the prompt without evidence, so CI output never arrives with
instruction authority and is sent once. It is never in state, the plan, the
request digest, a receipt or stdout. The finding keeps its prefix and only
drops "; no CI log text is available", so R80 (the rollback target) parses
every request and cell carrier this generation writes
(tests/host/ci-evidence-r80_test.ts, guarded on R80 being present). A
collection failure (permission, rate limit, timeout, expired or oversized
log, no spare time) adds one trusted reason line to the prompt and one plain
stderr line; it never blocks, refuses or re-charges the cell.

Every hosted implementation prompt also tells the implementer to read the
repository's CI workflows and run the fast checks that apply to its changes
when they work offline; the host never runs the target's toolchain. The
model sandbox has no network and no installed dependencies, so for
ai.ubq.fi (prettier, eslint and tsc from node_modules) the model cannot run
those gates itself; a trusted read-only dependency tree is a separate owner
decision.

Launcher part: the matrix_cell App token mint adds
`permission-actions: read` (.github/workflows/supervisor.yml). Today that
token has no Actions permission, so a foreign target's jobs and logs answer
403; without the grant the evidence degrades to identity and annotations
with the reason `forbidden_actions_read`. The App already holds Actions
write on ai.ubq.fi (run 39063997413 attempt 2 was re-run by
ubiquity-sentinel[bot]).

